@@ -0,0 +1,207 @@
+From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
+Subject: [PATCH] target/sparc: halt the CPU in Linux 3.2's idle loop on sun4u
+
+Debian 7's Linux 3.2 spins in its idle loop on sun4u and keeps a host
+core busy for as long as the VM runs. Its idle loop is older than the
+generic one the Linux layouts so far follow: cpu_idle() in
+arch/sparc/kernel/process_64.c spins with interrupts enabled (%pil 0)
+while need_resched() is clear, and its sparc64_yield() only touches the
+NMI watchdog on sun4u (it yields to the hypervisor on sun4v, after
+clearing TIF_POLLING_NRFLAG).
+
+The translator now also recognises that loop at its call of
+touch_nmi_watchdog(), as gcc 4.6 lays it out in Debian's sparc64 and
+sparc64-smp kernels: the test of tlb_type against hypervisor (3), the
+be,pn to the sun4v branch with mov 14, %o0 (TIF_POLLING_NRFLAG) in its
+delay slot, the call, the branch back to the top and the sun4v branch's
+call of clear_bit() with &current_thread_info()->flags. The Linux helper
+takes the interrupt level the loop runs the call at, 14 for the later
+kernels and 0 for 3.2, and halts the CPU there when TIF_NEED_RESCHED is
+clear in the flags at %g6 + 8, PSTATE.IE is set and nothing is pending,
+after the same 100 us of spinning as for the other guests. The CPU goes
+on at the call after the next interrupt.
+
+An idle Debian 7 at the shell then uses 0.1% of a host core instead of
+100% (the kernel is tickless when idle, CONFIG_NO_HZ); a CPU-bound job
+(13.4 s with the halt, 12.8 and 12.9 s without), ten "sleep 1" (10.4 s)
+and two minutes of CPU and file load take as long as before, and the
+guest clock keeps time.
