index 0000000..5d4abb7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/debian/patches/wsh/0114-target-sparc-halt-the-CPU-in-FreeBSD-s-idle-loop-on-.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,252 @@
+From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
+Subject: [PATCH] target/sparc: halt the CPU in FreeBSD's idle loop on sun4u
+
+FreeBSD 12.4 on sun4u spins in its idle loop and keeps a host core at
+100% for as long as the VM runs: the ULE scheduler's idle thread
+(sched_idletd()) calls cpu_idle(), which is empty on sparc64.
+
+The translator recognises the call in sched_idletd() as gcc 4.2 lays it
+out (FreeBSD 12.4-RELEASE GENERIC, at 0xc068f230): tdq_cpu_idle set,
+a membar, the test of tdq_load that branches to the call when it is 0,
+tdq_cpu_idle cleared again on both ways, the registers checked against
+each other. The word is tdq_load.
+
+Two things differ from the kernels 0042 handles:
+
+- FreeBSD's kernel runs at TL 1 (the user process's context stays in
+  the primary context register), so its idle loop is halted at TL 1;
+  the other kernels are still halted at TL 0 only.
+- Between two calls the loop looks for work to steal on other CPUs and
+  may spin for it itself (sched_idlespins), longer than the 20 us that
+  the 100 us poll allows between rounds, so the poll never ended and
+  the CPU was never halted. The loop is halted at the first call
+  instead; once woken, the call and one round of the loop run before
+  the next halt, as after a wake at the poll.
+
+The helper takes these as flags of the kernel (SPARC64_IDLE_TL1,
+SPARC64_IDLE_NO_POLL). -trace sparc64_idle_match shows "FreeBSD".
+
