diff --git a/debian/patches/wsh/0126-hw-audio-cs4231-clear-i24-s-pi-ci-and-ti-on-a-status.patch b/debian/patches/wsh/0126-hw-audio-cs4231-clear-i24-s-pi-ci-and-ti-on-a-status.patch
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..287aa1a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/debian/patches/wsh/0126-hw-audio-cs4231-clear-i24-s-pi-ci-and-ti-on-a-status.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
+Subject: [PATCH] hw/audio/cs4231: clear i24's pi, ci and ti on a status
+ register write
+
+The CS4231 datasheet has a write to the status register (R2) clear all
+of the interrupt flags: INT there and PI, CI and TI in I24, the
+alternate feature status register. QEMU's ISA model does that
+(hw/audio/cs4231a.c, case Status). "hw/audio/cs4231: set the codec's
+int and pi with the apc's playback interrupts" cleared INT only, so after a status write I24 still read PI, and a
+driver that wrote the status register to acknowledge an interrupt and
+then looked at I24 saw a PI that it had already handled.
+
+A write to the status register now clears PI, CI and TI in I24 as
+well as INT. The interrupt line is unchanged: it follows the APC CSR
+only, as before.
+
+No vmstate change: the bits are in regs and dregs.
+
+Tested in a sparc-softmmu build on Linux: sun4m-cs4231-test passes
+with the next commit's check, which fails without this change.
+---
+ hw/audio/cs4231.c | 13 +++++++++----
+ 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)
+
+diff --git a/hw/audio/cs4231.c b/hw/audio/cs4231.c
+index 91ac83e..9edab14 100644
+--- a/hw/audio/cs4231.c
++++ b/hw/audio/cs4231.c
+@@ -63,9 +63,10 @@
+  * I24, are set with the APC's playback interrupts (PI and PMI), not by
+  * the codec's playback counter (I14/I15, IEN in I10), which is not
+  * modelled. Linux reads INT before it looks at the APC CSR and ignores
+- * the interrupt without it; it clears the flags by writing I24. SunOS
+- * and Solaris never clear them, so they do not drive the interrupt
+- * line, which follows the APC CSR only.
++ * the interrupt without it; it clears the flags by writing I24, and a
++ * write to the status register clears them all. SunOS and Solaris never
++ * clear them, so they do not drive the interrupt line, which follows
++ * the APC CSR only.
+  *
+  * Capture is not done: its registers keep what is written, and the
+  * capture pipe always reads as empty.
+@@ -695,7 +696,11 @@ static void cs_mem_write(void *opaque, hwaddr addr,
+         }
+         break;
+     case CS_STATUS:
+-        /* a write clears INT; the other bits are read only */
++        /*
++         * A write clears INT and PI, CI and TI in I24, as the datasheet
++         * says; the other bits are read only
++         */
++        s->dregs[CS_IRQSTAT] &= ~CS_IRQ_ALL;
+         s->regs[CS_STATUS] &= ~CS_INT;
+         break;
+     case CS_CSR:
