index 0000000..0db198f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/debian/patches/wsh/0133-hw-sparc64-migrate-the-sabre-the-IOMMU-the-ebus-and-.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,244 @@
+From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
+Subject: [PATCH] hw/sparc64: migrate the sabre, the IOMMU, the ebus and the
+ CPU's interrupts
+
+Nothing in sun4u's interrupt path had migration state: the sabre host
+bridge had no VMState at all, nor did the sun4u IOMMU, the ebus or the
+sabre's own PCI function, and the sparc64 CPU's section leaves out
+SOFTINT and the interrupt vector it holds. After a migration, or a
+loadvm (which resets the machine before it loads the state), the
+machine ran (the CPU in the guest's idle loop, its timers going) but no
+interrupt from the on-board devices reached the CPU: the interrupt
+mapping registers came back with their valid bits clear (the guest's
+IMR writes from boot were lost), the request the sabre was waiting to
+have cleared was forgotten, and the serial console never answered
+again. With a disk the IOMMU came back off, so the IDE controller's DMA
+would have gone to the DVMA address taken as a physical address; and
+the ebus's BAR0 (which OpenBSD reads) was unmapped, its command
+register back at reset.
+
+This gives the sabre a VMState (the PCI and OBIO interrupt mapping
+registers, the lines that are up, the outstanding request, the PCI
+control registers and the reset status), the IOMMU one (its registers),
+the ebus and the sabre's PCI function vmstate_pci_device (their PCI
+configuration space, as the simbas behind the sabre already have), and
+the sparc64 CPU a subsection "cpu/sparc64_irq" with SOFTINT (where
+Linux takes its tick and stick timer interrupts), the interrupt vector
+(ivec_status, ivec_data) and idle_halted (wsh/0042's halt in the idle
+loop of a guest that polls for work itself), sent only when one is set.
+After loading it the CPU's pending interrupt is worked out again.
+
+sun4u has no versioned machine types, so there is no compat property,
+and the streams are not compatible either way. A stream from a QEMU
+without this no longer loads ("Unknown section or instance 'PCIHost'":
+the sabre's section was the generic PCI host bridge's, with nothing of
+the sabre's in it); resumed, such a stream gave a machine without
+interrupts anyway. A stream from this QEMU does not load into an older
+one ("Unknown section or instance 'sun4u-iommu'"); a live migration to
+an older QEMU fails and the source keeps running.
+
+The browser build (web/) carried the sabre and CPU parts as its own
+patch since its sun4u snapshot needed them; this takes them into the
+series with the IOMMU, the ebus, the sabre's PCI function and
+idle_halted added.
+
+Tested on Linux, qemu-system-sparc64 from the pve-qemu tree, each guest
+saved with savevm and restored by loadvm, by a migration to a file
+loaded with -incoming into a new process and by -loadvm in a new
+process: wisp's shell (Linux 7.3.4) answers, its uptime advancing and
+crc 64 right; Debian 7 (Linux 3.2) and FreeBSD 12.4 from an IDE disk
+answer, sleep 2 takes 2-3 s, disk reads give the same md5 as at boot
+and a ping to the user network's gateway gets every reply. Without
+this patch the console is silent after each restore. Through PVE,
+Debian 7 passes qm snapshot --vmstate 1 with qm rollback, and qm
+suspend --todisk with qm start. "info pci" and the memory map are the
+same on both sides of a migration. The next patch adds a qtest.
