--- /dev/null
+++ b/debian/patches/wsh/0147-target-sparc-keep-the-SuperSPARC-cache-tag-and-data-.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,351 @@
+From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
+Subject: [PATCH] target/sparc: keep the SuperSPARC cache tag and data RAMs
+
+After the store buffer and MXCC tests, Sun's SPARCserver 600MP boot PROM
+tests the SuperSPARC's instruction and data cache RAMs through the
+diagnostic ASIs 0x0c-0x0f (I-cache tag, I-cache data, D-cache tag,
+D-cache data): it writes patterns to every set tag, physical tag and
+data doubleword and reads them back, and loops on the first mismatch
+("wrong i$ stag"). QEMU ignores stores to those ASIs and reads them as 0.
+
+Add the CPU feature "cache-diag", set on the TI-SuperSparc-* models.
+With it the ASIs reach RAMs with the TMS390Z50's geometry: the I-cache
+has 5 ways of 64 sets with 64-byte lines, the D-cache 4 ways of 128 sets
+with 32-byte lines. The way is in address bits 28-26, the set in bits
+11-6 (I-cache) or 11-5 (D-cache), the doubleword in the bits below; bit
+31 selects the physical tag and bit 30 the set tag, as Linux's
+asm/viking.h and SunOS 4.1.x use them. Tags keep the bits the PROM
+tests (set tag 0x1f1e for the I-cache, 0xf0e for the D-cache; physical
+tag 0x0300000000ffffff and 0x0101010000ffffff, with the valid, dirty
+and shared bits Linux's viking.h names); an address that selects no
+entry reads 0 and ignores stores. A word access reads or writes one half
+of a doubleword. QEMU still doesn't cache through them: no hits, misses
+or flash clear. The microSPARC, TurboSPARC and LEON models are
+unchanged, and -cpu TI-SuperSparc-II,-cache-diag turns it off.
+
+The RAMs (49 KB) go in a vmstate subsection "cpu/cache_diag", sent only
+when they are not all zero. OpenBIOS doesn't write them; SunOS 4.1.x
+writes the D-cache tags and data in its workaround for a Viking erratum,
+so its streams carry the subsection.
+
+With this the PROM passes its I- and D-cache RAM tests, and on the
+TI-SuperSparc-II its MXCC external cache RAM test is next.
