+++ b/debian/patches/wsh/0173-hw-net-lance-receive-loopback-frames-as-the-Am7990-o.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,172 @@
+From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
+Subject: [PATCH] hw/net/lance: receive loopback frames as the Am7990 on the
+ SS-600MP
+
+Sun's SPARCserver 600MP boot PROM tests the lance in internal loopback
+(progress 0xc4 of its power-on self test, a loop on error otherwise):
+in its buffer memory it sets up one receive descriptor with a 40-byte
+buffer and one transmit descriptor with a 32-byte frame, starts the
+lance and waits for the receive descriptor to come back. Two things in
+the PCnet core behind QEMU's lance kept it from coming back:
+
+- The PROM leaves in the receive descriptor's message count word (RMD3)
+  what its memory test wrote there (0xa5a5). QEMU's descriptor check
+  wants bits 15-12 of that word zero and otherwise takes the ring as
+  having no descriptor, so the frame was missed (CSR0 MISS and ERR).
+  The Am7990 doesn't check that word: it writes it itself, with its
+  reserved bits as zeros.
+- QEMU sends a loopback frame back through qemu_receive_packet(), which
+  pads frames shorter than 60 bytes, so the lance received 64 bytes
+  (60 and the FCS) for 32 sent, overflowed the 40-byte buffer (OFLO,
+  BUFF, ERR) and wrote no message count. The Am7990 receives a frame in
+  loopback at the length it sent it: 36 bytes with the FCS.
+
+New lance property am7990 (default off) and hwdef field le_am7990, set
+on the SS-600MP only: the check of RMD3's reserved bits is skipped,
+those bits are written as zeros with the message count, and a loopback
+frame is received at its transmitted length. The other sun4m machines
+keep the old receive path on purpose: the SS-5 and SS-20 boot PROMs'
+own loopback test then fails ("Wrong packet length; expected 36,
+observed 64") and, with a blank NVRAM (diag-switch? true, boot from the
+network), they give up and reach "ok"; with the Am7990 behaviour their
+test passes and they wait for a RARP reply that never comes, as a real
+machine would. The PCI PCnet is unchanged; nothing changes for frames
+from the network; no new state.
